**CONFIRMED.** Two halves, both reproduced:

**(b) `smeltMode`** — the sharper one, and a genuine defect. `osaurus_settings.TRACKED_KEYS` covers 23 keys over exactly two filenames and holds no `smeltMode`. The ad hoc reader `osaurus_smelt_mode` is called only at `runtimes.py:2654`, *after* `if stream_experts is None: return None` at `:2642-2643` — so a cell with no `--stream-experts` pin never asks the host anything. All three grid runners leave that flag out; only `run_sweep_streaming.sh` names it. A capture at `disabled` and one at `ssdStreaming` diff to `()`.

**(a) the plist** — real as an absence. Nothing in the package reads `~/Library/Preferences/com.dinoki.osaurus.plist`; `TRACKED_KEYS` has no plist surface. AGENTS.md:92's "and an app plist" is unenforced. But the runtime doc contradicts its own mechanism: a 0.25.18 probe (`docs/runtimes/osaurus.md:60-64`) found `modelOptions.disableThinking` **inert** — identical 1,706-character reasoning. Different delivery path (request option vs stored preference), so not strictly contradictory, but it's the repo's only behavioural probe and it found nothing. I've flagged that rather than repeat the finding's confidence.

I found three things worth your attention: `runtimes.py:2648` cites `docs/runtimes/osaurus.md:298` for the smelt enum, but that line is inside the `osaurus bench` usage block — the enum is at `:375`. The host sits at `smeltMode: "disabled"` today (schema 5; the harness's backup is schema 3), so (b) is latent, not active. And none of the 13 measured models across 303 published Osaurus rows is one of the three with `disableThinking` on — though 2,394 of 3,016 observations do carry `reasoning_text`, which partly mitigates (a) since a reader can see reasoning occurred.

Verification: `tests/test_fable_s5_f9.py`, 2 failed / 7 passed, deterministic across four runs. Red-check confirmed by appending the one key to `TRACKED_KEYS` in memory — it turns green, and absent again on restoration. Suite baseline I started from: 70 failed / 1,391 passed. That count is not stable because this worktree is shared — I caught a parallel verifier's temporary edit to `_run` in `runtimes.py` at 17:58 (since reverted), which explains the two transiently-failing `test_runtimes.py` tests; both pass with the source clean.

